# Line endings are load-bearing here, not cosmetic.
#
# Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrote
# scripts/seed-demo.sh with CRLF. demo/Dockerfile.seed COPYs that file into a
# Linux image, where bash reads the trailing \r as part of each token:
#
#   /app/seed-demo.sh: line 10: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#   /app/seed-demo.sh: line 24: syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
#
# The seed then exits 2, and `web` depends on it completing, so the demo the
# README leads with never came up at all — on Windows only, which is why a
# Linux CI run stayed green.
* text=auto

# Anything a Linux shell, Docker or nginx has to read must be LF, on every
# platform, regardless of the cloner's git config.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf

# And the Windows launchers must stay CRLF: a .bat with LF endings misbehaves
# on some Windows versions in ways that look like the file being corrupt.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
